      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The comparison directly identifies the recital and Attachment A's insufficiency language, making the asserted conflict quick to compare with d01. The added argument that the recital could control the schedule is readily separable from that conflict."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description isolates the choice conveyed by 'any or all,' whereas d02 concerns whether forbearance was Aurora's only obligation in light of the repayment provisions. Identifying those different interpretive questions requires no factual verification."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look at paragraphs 7 and 8 establishes the provisions the description invokes and their qualifications. The comparison then turns on an identifiable distinction: preservation of foreclosure rights versus d02's relationship between forbearance and the repayment promises."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The asserted problem is a concrete discrepancy between the first-payment date and the receipt deadline, readily located in Attachment A and the cover letter. Its subject is plainly distinguishable from d03's repayment-purpose versus payment-insufficiency tension."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "The reviewer needs one additional look to separate paragraph 4's reference to full reinstatement from the expressly identified recital–insufficiency conflict. The latter gives a concrete comparison with d04, without requiring resolution of the broader rhetorical claim that the plan could 'never cure' the default."
    }
  ]
}
